## The properties (the commission's, in measured form; they replace B1658's P4 and P5 where they conflict)
Frames: Partner, `/revenue`, steps 1, 3 and 4 (step 2 has no unit box), at 1280 px and at 390 px viewport width. A "box" is the grey unit span (id ending `-unit`); the "number box" is the input beside it.
- **P1** all 16 grey boxes have one rendered width (spread at most 0.5 px). **P3** within each step the inputs' left edges are equal and so are the grey boxes' left edges.
- **Q1** at 1280 every number box is at least 1.5 times as wide as its grey box ("the NUMBER box is wider than the grey box, by at least half the grey box's width").
- **Q2** the label column is not narrower than at base main: at 1280 every hint paragraph (`p` with id ending `-hint`) is at least 203 px wide (base main measures 203.2 px, `5f6b916b` measures 171.2 px) and, by program, every label and hint has the SAME line count at the new head as at base main (96 rows over 1280, 820 and 390 px, S08).
- **Q3** every unit text is fully visible: at most 2 lines; every line of the text lies inside the box's content area (no clipping, no ellipsis); `scrollWidth` not over `clientWidth`; and every box is as tall as its input (the input's own `min-h-11`, S07: 44.0 px on all 48 candidate rows, and at base on every row but the two three-line long-unit rows, which grew to 50.0, S08) with all 16 boxes one height, so no row is ragged. A long unit (`USD / hour`, `USD / device / month`) MAY take two lines only on that condition.
- **P6** no word or figure changes (by program, Step 5). Smaller unit text is allowed; no word change.

## The design (DERIVED from the sweep below; the words are the drafter's; the numbers are MEASURED)
**Choice, with its evidence.** The control column goes back to `14rem` (224 px, base's own value, so Q2 holds by construction and was measured: 96 of 96 rows equal, 0 differ). Q1 then caps the grey box: 224 minus box at least 1.5 times box gives box at most 89.6 px. Q3 floors it: at 12 px text (the base `text-xs`) with 8 px side padding `USD / device / month` needs more than 89.6 px, so the text becomes 11 px (`text-[0.6875rem]`, line height `leading-4` = 16 px) and the side padding 6 px (`px-1.5`). On the drafter's machine the smallest width meeting Q3 in the system font is 82 px (2 lines, 0.8 px to spare); in the widest of six stress fonts tried (Verdana, which the drafter takes as a stand-in for a wide Linux fallback on the hosted runner: DERIVED, no hosted font was read) it is 86 px (0.5 px to spare), S12 and S13. **The chosen box is 86 px = `w-[5.375rem]`**: the smallest width that holds in all seven fonts. The number box is then 138.0 px (ratio 1.60). Q-2 asks Tuesday about 88 px. This is MEASURED, not PREDICTED: the drafter built the exact candidate (below) and measured it, see S-lines; the seat re-measures.
**The edit, TWO lines of `RevenueCalculator.tsx` (base `:201` and `:220`; at `5f6b916b` they are the same two lines).** (a) `:201` (S05): `md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_16rem]` becomes `md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_14rem]` (the very text base main has). (b) `:220` (S07): the class string of the suffix span becomes exactly `flex w-[5.375rem] shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-r-md border border-l-0 border-border-strong bg-surface-sunken px-1.5 text-center text-[0.6875rem] leading-4 text-fg-muted` (the old `w-40`, `whitespace-nowrap` and `px-2 text-xs` are gone: `whitespace-nowrap` MUST go or `USD / device / month` cannot take its second line and spills 18.7 px out of the box). Nothing else on either line. The drafter made this edit in a scratch copy and `diff` printed exactly those two lines (S-lines); the Tailwind build emits all four new classes (a count of 1 each in the built CSS, control: 0 in the `5f6b916b` build, S10) and the computed styles read 11px, 16px line height, 6px side padding, centred, `whitespace` normal, grid columns `203.188px 224px` at 1280 (S11).
**What it looks like (MEASURED; the drafter read its own screenshots of the candidate).** Step 1 at 1280: six grey boxes of one width, one line each, `USD / hour` on ONE line; the number box clearly wider than the grey one; labels wrap exactly as at base. Step 4: `USD / device / month` shows on two lines (`USD / device` over `/ month`), the box and its input both 44 px tall. The product owner sees your own screenshots before anything lands.
**The sweep behind the choice (MEASURED, injected CSS on a local `5f6b916b` build, the drafter's `sweep.txt` and `fontsweep.txt`, S12 and S13).** 12 px with 8 px padding passes Q3 only from 90 px, which breaks Q1 (input 134, ratio 1.49); 11 px with 8 px padding passes from 86 px; 11 px with 6 px padding from 82 px; 10 px with 6 px padding from 76 px. 11 px was taken as the middle: 10 px is harder to read and its extra room is not needed (Q-1 asks Tuesday).

